 improvements the company made within a year.' AND sp.marks IS 6 AND sp.correct_answer IS 'It tarred the road, drilled two boreholes, and started a night class for young apprentices.')) AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM structured_question_parts sp WHERE sp.id = 'sqp_q_eng_2024_2_007_d' AND NOT (sp.question_id IS 'q_eng_2024_2_007' AND sp.part_label IS 'd' AND sp.part_text IS 'Why did the grumbling residents eventually soften their complaint?' AND sp.marks IS 6 AND sp.correct_answer IS 'Because their children could now learn a trade at home instead of travelling to the city.')) AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM structured_question_parts sp WHERE sp.id = 'sqp_q_eng_2024_2_007_e' AND NOT (sp.question_id IS 'q_eng_2024_2_007' AND sp.part_label IS 'e' AND sp.part_text IS 'What is the meaning of the word "apprentices" as used in the passage?' AND sp.marks IS 4 AND sp.correct_answer IS 'Young people learning a trade or skill under training.')) AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM structured_question_parts sp WHERE sp.id = 'sqp_q_eng_2024_2_007_f' AND NOT (sp.question_id IS 'q_eng_2024_2_007' AND sp.part_label IS 'f' AND sp.part_text IS 'What is the writer''s attitude towards the assembly plant?' AND sp.marks IS 4 AND sp.correct_answer IS 'The writer is balanced but broadly positive, acknowledging early problems while emphasizing the benefits.')) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END;
INSERT INTO _migration_363_guard(valid) SELECT CASE WHEN NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM questions q WHERE q.id = 'q_eng_2024_2_008' AND NOT (q.topic_id IS 'topic_wassce_p2_eng_sum' AND q.subject_id IS 'subj_wassce_english' AND q.exam_type_id IS 'exam_wassce' AND q.paper_type_id IS 'paper_wassce_2' AND q.past_paper_id IS 'pp_wassce_eng_2024_2' AND q.question_text IS 'Read the passage carefully and answer ALL the questions that follow. — "Urban gardening is quietly changing Accra. On balconies, in gutters laid along walls, and in sacks filled with compost, residents now grow pepper, tomatoes and aleefu. The practice began as a response to rising food prices, but its rewards reach further. Households spend less on vegetables, and surplus harvests are sold to neighbours. Rooftop beds also cool buildings, and the greenery softens the concrete skyline. Health workers add another gain: families who grow their own vegetables eat them more often, and children who tend the plants learn where food comes from."' AND q.question_type IS 'structured' AND q.round_type IS NULL AND q.options IS NULL AND q.correct_answer IS '(a) Urban gardening began in Accra as a response to rising food prices. (b) Economically, households spend less on vegetables and can sell surplus harvests to neighbours; environmentally, rooftop beds cool buildings and the greenery softens the skyline. (c) Families who grow vegetables eat them more often, and children who tend the plants learn where food comes from.' AND q.explanation IS '(a) Urban gardening began in Accra as a response to rising food prices. (b) Economically, households spend less on vegetables and can sell surplus harvests to neighbours; environmentally, rooftop beds cool buildings and the greenery softens the skyline. (c) Families who grow vegetables eat them more often, and children who tend the plants learn where food comes from.' AND q.difficulty IS 'medium' AND q.points IS '30' AND q.marks IS '30' AND q.time_limit IS NULL AND q.question_number IS '8' AND q.section IS 'C' AND q.is_compulsory IS '1' AND q.image_url IS NULL AND q.syllabus_topic_id IS NULL AND q.command_word IS 'Summarize' AND q.assessment_objective IS 'AO2' AND q.source_paper_code IS NULL AND q.source_question_number IS NULL AND q.exam_board_id IS NULL)) AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM question_content_releases r WHERE r.question_id = 'q_eng_2024_2_008' AND NOT (r.batch_id IS 'wassce-paper2-theory-001' AND r.quality_assurance IS 'automated_beta' AND r.release_channel IS 'beta' AND r.content_label IS 'Original BrillaPrep curriculum-aligned WASSCE English Language Paper 2 theory practice; not official WAEC examination material. Use the enabled feedback channel to report corrections.' AND r.source_url IS 'https://waecgh.org/' AND r.official_exam_board_content IS 0 AND r.feedback_enabled IS 1)) AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM structured_question_parts sp WHERE sp.id = 'sqp_q_eng_2024_2_008_a' AND NOT (sp.question_id IS 'q_eng_2024_2_008' AND sp.part_label IS 'a' AND sp.part_text IS 'In ONE sentence, state why urban gardening began in Accra, according to the passage.' AND sp.marks IS 10 AND sp.correct_answer IS 'Urban gardening began in Accra as a response to rising food prices.')) AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM structured_question_parts sp WHERE sp.id = 'sqp_q_eng_2024_2_008_b' AND NOT (sp.question_id IS 'q_eng_2024_2_008' AND sp.part_label IS 'b' AND sp.part_text IS 'In TWO sentences, summarize the economic and environmental rewards of urban gardening mentioned in the passage.' AND sp.marks IS 10 AND sp.correct_answer IS 'Economically, households spend less on vegetables and can sell surplus harvests to neighbours; environmentally, rooftop beds cool buildings and the greenery softens the skyline.')) AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM structured_question_parts sp WHERE sp.id = 'sqp_q_eng_2024_2_008_c' AND NOT (sp.question_id IS 'q_eng_2024_2_008' AND sp.part_label IS 'c' AND sp.part_text IS 'In ONE sentence, state the health and educational benefit the passage attributes to urban gardening.' AND sp.marks IS 10 AND sp.correct_answer IS 'Families who grow vegetables eat them more often, and children who tend the plants learn where food comes from.')) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END;
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO past_papers (id, exam_type_id, subject_id, paper_type_id, year, month, series, title, description, total_questions, total_marks, time_allowed, instructions, is_complete, is_premium, source_url) VALUES ('pp_wassce_eng_2024_2', 'exam_wassce', 'subj_wassce_english', 'paper_wassce_2', '2024', 'May-June', 'WASSCE', 'English Language Paper 2 (2024)', 'WASSCE English Language theory practice: essay, comprehension and summary.', '8', '300', '120', 'Answer ONE question from Section A and ALL the questions in Sections B and C.', '1', '0', NULL);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, slug, description, display_order) VALUES ('topic_wassce_p2_eng_ess', 'subj_wassce_english', 'Essay writing', 'wassce-p2-eng-ess', 'Compose sustained, well-organized essays in the register and format each essay type demands.', 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, slug, description, display_order) VALUES ('topic_wassce_p2_eng_cmp', 'subj_wassce_english', 'Comprehension', 'wassce-p2-eng-cmp', 'Read an unseen passage closely and answer literal, inferential and vocabulary questions on it.', 2);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, slug, description, display_order) VALUES ('topic_wassce_p2_eng_sum', 'subj_wassce_english', 'Summary writing', 'wassce-p2-eng-sum', 'Distil a passage into concise sentences that capture the required points without distortion.', 3);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id, topic_id, subject_id, exam_type_id, paper_type_id, past_paper_id, question_text, question_type, round_type, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, points, marks, time_limit, question_number, section, is_compulsory, image_url, syllabus_topic_id, command_word, assessment_objective, source_paper_code, source_question_number, exam_board_id) VALUES ('q_eng_2024_2_001', 'topic_wassce_p2_eng_ess', 'subj_wassce_english', 'exam_wassce', 'paper_wassce_2', 'pp_wassce_eng_2024_2', 'Write a story that ends with the words: ''...so that was how Grandfather''s old bicycle saved the entire harvest.''', 'essay', NULL, NULL, 'A strong response builds a clear narrative arc: an impending threat to the harvest (drought, pests or flood), the narrator''s failing attempts to help, and the old bicycle becoming the unexpected means of salvation — perhaps carrying water from a distant well trip after trip, or rushing a message that brings help. The ending sentence must arise naturally from the plot rather than feel bolted on. The best answers sustain past-tense narration, use vivid but controlled description, develop one or two rounded characters, and keep spelling and punctuation secure throughout.', 'A strong response builds a clear narrative arc: an impending threat to the harvest (drought, pests or flood), the narrator''s failing attempts to help, and the old bicycle becoming the unexpected means of salvation — perhaps carrying water from a distant well trip after trip, or rushing a message that brings help. The ending sentence must arise naturally from the plot rather than feel bolted on. The best answers sustain past-tense narration, use vivid but controlled description, develop one or two rounded characters, and keep spelling and punctuation secure throughout.', 'medium', '40', '40', NULL, '1', 'A', '0', NULL, NULL, 'Write', 'AO2', NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id, topic_id, subject_id, exam_type_id, paper_type_id, past_paper_id, question_text, question_type, round_type, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, points, marks, time_limit, question_number, section, is_compulsory, image_url, syllabus_topic_id, command_word, assessment_objective, source_paper_code, source_question_number, exam_board_id) VALUES ('q_eng_2024_2_002', 'topic_wassce_p2_eng_ess', 'subj_wassce_english', 'exam_wassce', 'paper_wassce_2', 'pp_wassce_eng_2024_2', 'Write an article for your school magazine on the topic: ''Mobile phones in the classroom: helpful tool or harmful distraction?''', 'essay', NULL, NULL, 'A strong article takes a clear position while acknowledging the other side. It opens with a headline and an engaging introduction, presents two or three developed points — for example, instant access to reference material and educational apps versus loss of attention, cheating and cyber-distraction — supports each with a concrete classroom illustration, and closes with a recommendation such as supervised, purpose-bound phone use. Register should be semi-formal and lively, addressed to fellow students, with a byline and headline marking the article format.', 'A strong article takes a clear position while acknowledging the other side. It opens with a headline and an engaging introduction, presents two or three developed points — for example, instant access to reference material and educational apps versus loss of attention, cheating and cyber-distraction — supports each with a concrete classroom illustration, and closes with a recommendation such as supervised, purpose-bound phone use. Register should be semi-formal and lively, addressed to fellow students, with a byline and headline marking the article format.', 'medium', '40', '40', NULL, '2', 'A', '0', NULL, NULL, 'Write', 'AO2', NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id, topic_id, subject_id, exam_type_id, paper_type_id, past_paper_id, question_text, question_type, round_type, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, points, marks, time_limit, question_number, section, is_compulsory, image_url, syllabus_topic_id, command_word, assessment_objective, source_paper_code, source_question_number, exam_board_id) VALUES ('q_eng_2024_2_003', 'topic_wassce_p2_eng_ess', 'subj_wassce_english', 'exam_wassce', 'paper_wassce_2', 'pp_wassce_eng_2024_2', 'Write a letter to the District Chief Executive of your area explaining three causes of poor sanitation in your community and suggesting two ways the Assembly can help solve the problem.', 'essay', NULL, NULL, 'A strong answer uses full formal-letter format: writer''s address, date, recipient''s designation and address, salutation, a heading in capitals, body paragraphs and a formal subscription. The body identifies three distinct causes — for example, irregular refuse collection, too few public waste bins, and open drains that residents misuse — and links each to observable effects such as cholera outbreaks or flooding. It then makes two actionable suggestions, such as a scheduled collection service and a community bin programme with by-law enforcement. Tone is respectful and persuasive throughout.', 'A strong answer uses full formal-letter format: writer''s address, date, recipient''s designation and address, salutation, a heading in capitals, body paragraphs and a formal subscription. The body identifies three distinct causes — for example, irregular refuse collection, too few public waste bins, and open drains that residents misuse — and links each to observable effects such as cholera outbreaks or flooding. It then makes two actionable suggestions, such as a scheduled collection service and a community bin programme with by-law enforcement. Tone is respectful and persuasive throughout.', 'medium', '40', '40', NULL, '3', 'A', '0', NULL, NULL, 'Write', 'AO2', NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id, topic_id, subject_id, exam_type_id, paper_type_id, past_paper_id, question_text, question_type, round_type, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, points, marks, time_limit, question_number, section, is_compulsory, image_url, syllabus_topic_id, command_word, assessment_objective, source_paper_code, source_question_number, exam_board_id) VALUES ('q_eng_2024_2_004', 'topic_wassce_p2_eng_ess', 'subj_wassce_english', 'exam_wassce', 'paper_wassce_2', 'pp_wassce_eng_2024_2', 'As the principal speaker for your school''s debate club, write your speech for or against the motion: ''Day schooling is better than boarding schooling for senior high students.''', 'essay', NULL, NULL, 'A strong debate speech opens with the vocative address (Chairperson, Panel of Judges, Ladies and Gentlemen) and a firm declaration of the side taken. It then advances three or four distinct arguments — for the motion: parental supervision, lower cost, freedom from hostel distractions; against it: structured prep time, independence, peer learning — each developed with an example or appeal to the audience''s experience. Rhetorical questions, rebuttal of the opposing side''s strongest claim, and a rousing conclusion mark the best speeches. Persuasive, spirited register is sustained without sliding into insult.', 'A strong debate speech opens with the vocative address (Chairperson, Panel of Judges, Ladies and Gentlemen) and a firm declaration of the side taken. It then advances three or four distinct arguments — for the motion: parental supervision, lower cost, freedom from hostel distractions; against it: structured prep time, independence, peer learning — each developed with an example or appeal to the audience''s experience. Rhetorical questions, rebuttal of the opposing side''s strongest claim, and a rousing conclusion mark the best speeches. Persuasive, spirited register is sustained without sliding into insult.', 'medium', '40', '40', NULL, '4', 'A', '0', NULL, NULL, 'Argue', 'AO2', NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id, topic_id, subject_id, exam_type_id, paper_type_id, past_paper_id, question_text, question_type, round_type, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, points, marks, time_limit, question_number, section, is_compulsory, image_url, syllabus_topic_id, command_word, assessment_objective, source_paper_code, source_question_number, exam_board_id) VALUES ('q_eng_2024_2_005', 'topic_wassce_p2_eng_ess', 'subj_wassce_english', 'exam_wassce', 'paper_wassce_2', 'pp_wassce_eng_2024_2', 'Describe a busy market in your town on the morning of a festival, and explain what the scene reveals about your community.', 'essay', NULL, NULL, 'A strong descriptive essay appeals to at least three senses: the colours of cloth stalls and produce, the noise of bargaining and festival drums, the smells of kelewele and fresh bread. It organizes the scene spatially — entrance, food section, cloth section — or chronologically through the morning. The second half moves from description to interpretation: the scene shows communal solidarity, the importance of trade to livelihoods, or how tradition and commerce intertwine. The best answers use precise vocabulary, varied sentence openings and controlled imagery without purple overload.', 'A strong descriptive essay appeals to at least three senses: the colours of cloth stalls and produce, the noise of bargaining and festival drums, the smells of kelewele and fresh bread. It organizes the scene spatially — entrance, food section, cloth section — or chronologically through the morning. The second half moves from description to interpretation: the scene shows communal solidarity, the importance of trade to livelihoods, or how tradition and commerce intertwine. The best answers use precise vocabulary, varied sentence openings and controlled imagery without purple overload.', 'medium', '40', '40', NULL, '5', 'A', '0', NULL, NULL, 'Describe', 'AO2', NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id, topic_id, subject_id, exam_type_id, paper_type_id, past_paper_id, question_text, question_type, round_type, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, points, marks, time_limit, question_number, section, is_compulsory, image_url, syllabus_topic_id, command_word, assessment_objective, source_paper_code, source_question_number, exam_board_id) VALUES ('q_eng_2024_2_006', 'topic_wassce_p2_eng_ess', 'subj_wassce_english', 'exam_wassce', 'paper_wassce_2', 'pp_wassce_eng_2024_2', 'A friend in another school wants to join the reading club in your school. Write a letter to your friend describing the activities of the club and explaining two benefits of belonging to it.', 'essay', NULL, NULL, 'A strong informal letter carries the writer''s address, date, an affectionate salutation and a warm subscription with first name only. The body describes concrete club activities — weekly reading sessions, book reviews, debates on characters, author visits — and explains two benefits, such as improved vocabulary and comprehension for examinations, and confidence in public speaking from presenting reviews. The tone is friendly and enthusiastic, contractions are acceptable, and the letter ends by encouraging the friend to join or explaining how membership works.', 'A strong informal letter carries the writer''s address, date, an affectionate salutation and a warm subscription with first name only. The body describes concrete club activities — weekly reading sessions, book reviews, debates on characters, author visits — and explains two benefits, such as improved vocabulary and comprehension for examinations, and confidence in public speaking from presenting reviews. The tone is friendly and enthusiastic, contractions are acceptable, and the letter ends by encouraging the friend to join or explaining how membership works.', 'medium', '40', '40', NULL, '6', 'A', '0', NULL, NULL, 'Write', 'AO2', NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id, topic_id, subject_id, exam_type_id, paper_type_id, past_paper_id, question_text, question_type, round_type, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, points, marks, time_limit, question_number, section, is_compulsory, image_url, syllabus_topic_id, command_word, assessment_objective, source_paper_code, source_question_number, exam_board_id) VALUES ('q_eng_2024_2_007', 'topic_wassce_p2_eng_cmp', 'subj_wassce_english', 'exam_wassce', 'paper_wassce_2', 'pp_wassce_eng_2024_2', 'Read the passage carefully and answer ALL the questions that follow. — "When the assembly plant opened on the edge of town, Aba expected only jobs. What the town got first was dust. Trucks roared through the market road from dawn, and shopkeepers wiped their wares twice before noon. Within a year, however, the company had tarred the road, drilled two boreholes and started a night class for young apprentices. Some residents still grumbled that the noise never stopped, but even they admitted their children no longer travelled to the city to learn a trade."', 'structured', NULL, NULL, '(a) Aba expected the plant to bring only jobs (employment) to the town. (b) Dust from the trucks, and the noise of trucks roaring through the market road from dawn. (c) It tarred the road, drilled two boreholes, and started a night class for young apprentices. (d) Because their children could now learn a trade at home instead of travelling to the city. (e) Young people learning a trade or skill under training. (f) The writer is balanced but broadly positive, acknowledging early problems while emphasizing the benefits.', '(a) Aba expected the plant to bring only jobs (employment) to the town. (b) Dust from the trucks, and the noise of trucks roaring through the market road from dawn. (c) It tarred the road, drilled two boreholes, and started a night class for young apprentices. (d) Because their children could now learn a trade at home instead of travelling to the city. (e) Young people learning a trade or skill under training. (f) The writer is balanced but broadly positive, acknowledging early problems while emphasizing the benefits.', 'medium', '30', '30', NULL, '7', 'B', '1', NULL, NULL, 'Read', 'AO2', NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions (id, topic_id, subject_id, exam_type_id, paper_type_id, past_paper_id, question_text, question_type, round_type, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, points, marks, time_limit, question_number, section, is_compulsory, image_url, syllabus_topic_id, command_word, assessment_objective, source_paper_code, source_question_number, exam_board_id) VALUES ('q_eng_2024_2_008', 'topic_wassce_p2_eng_sum', 'subj_wassce_english', 'exam_wassce', 'paper_wassce_2', 'pp_wassce_eng_2024_2', 'Read the passage carefully and answer ALL the questions that follow. — "Urban gardening is quietly changing Accra. On balconies, in gutters laid along walls, and in sacks filled with compost, residents now grow pepper, tomatoes and aleefu. The practice began as a response to rising food prices, but its rewards reach further. Households spend less on vegetables, and surplus harvests are sold to neighbours. Rooftop beds also cool buildings, and the greenery softens the concrete skyline. Health workers add another gain: families who grow their own vegetables eat them more often, and children who tend the plants learn where food comes from."', 'structured', NULL, NULL, '(a) Urban gardening began in Accra as a response to rising food prices. (b) Economically, households spend less on vegetables and can sell surplus harvests to neighbours; environmentally, rooftop beds cool buildings and the greenery softens the skyline. (c) Families who grow vegetables eat them more often, and children who tend the plants learn where food comes from.', '(a) Urban gardening began in Accra as a response to rising food prices. (b) Economically, households spend less on vegetables and can sell surplus harvests to neighbours; environmentally, rooftop beds cool buildings and the greenery softens the skyline. (c) Families who grow vegetables eat them more often, and children who tend the plants learn where food comes from.', 'medium', '30', '30', NULL, '8', 'C', '1', NULL, NULL, 'Summarize', 'AO2', NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO essay_questions (id, question_id, word_limit_min, word_limit_max, requires_introduction, requires_conclusion, marking_scheme, model_answer, marking_rubric, required_points, optional_points, ai_grading_enabled) VALUES ('eq_q_eng_2024_2_001', 'q_eng_2024_2_001', '250', '600', '1', '1', '{"points":[{"point":"Content: relevance, depth and development of ideas","marks":10},{"point":"Organization: paragraphing, coherence and logical flow","marks":10},{"point":"Expression: register, vocabulary and sentence control","marks":15},{"point":"Mechanical accuracy: spelling, punctuation and grammar","marks":5}]}', 'A strong response builds a clear narrative arc: an impending threat to the harvest (drought, pests or flood), the narrator''s failing attempts to help, and the old bicycle becoming the unexpected means of salvation — perhaps carrying water from a distant well trip after trip, or rushing a message that brings help. The ending sentence must arise naturally from the plot rather than feel bolted on. The best answers sustain past-tense narration, use vivid but controlled description, develop one or two rounded characters, and keep spelling and punctuation secure throughout.', NULL, '["A narrative plot in which the bicycle plausibly saves the harvest","The required closing words used verbatim and earned by the story","Consistent past-tense narration with paragraphing"]', '["Dialogue that advances the plot","Sensory description of the farm setting","A moment of suspense before the resolution"]', '1');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO essay_questions (id, question_id, word_limit_min, word_limit_max, requires_introduction, requires_conclusion, marking_scheme, model_answer, marking_rubric, required_points, optional_points, ai_grading_enabled) VALUES ('eq_q_eng_2024_2_002', 'q_eng_2024_2_002', '250', '600', '1', '1', '{"points":[{"point":"Content: relevance, depth and development of ideas","marks":10},{"point":"Organization: paragraphing, coherence and logical flow","marks":10},{"point":"Expression: register, vocabulary and sentence control","marks":15},{"point":"Mechanical accuracy: spelling, punctuation and grammar","marks":5}]}', 'A strong article takes a clear position while acknowledging the other side. It opens with a headline and an engaging introduction, presents two or three developed points — for example, instant access to reference material and educational apps versus loss of attention, cheating and cyber-distraction — supports each with a concrete classroom illustration, and closes with a recommendation such as supervised, purpose-bound phone use. Register should be semi-formal and lively, addressed to fellow students, with a byline and headline marking the article format.', NULL, '["Article format: headline and, ideally, a byline","A clear, defensible position on the question","At least two developed supporting points with classroom illustrations"]', '["Acknowledgement of the opposing view","A practical recommendation in the conclusion","Rhetorical questions or direct address to engage readers"]', '1');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO essay_questions (id, question_id, word_limit_min, word_limit_max, requires_introduction, requires_conclusion, marking_scheme, model_answer, marking_rubric, required_points, optional_points, ai_grading_enabled) VALUES ('eq_q_eng_2024_2_003', 'q_eng_2024_2_003', '250', '600', '1', '1', '{"points":[{"point":"Content: relevance, depth and development of ideas","marks":10},{"point":"Organization: paragraphing, coherence and logical flow","marks":10},{"point":"Expression: register, vocabulary and sentence control","marks":15},{"point":"Mechanical accuracy: spelling, punctuation and grammar","marks":5}]}', 'A strong answer uses full formal-letter format: writer''s address, date, recipient''s designation and address, salutation, a heading in capitals, body paragraphs and a formal subscription. The body identifies three distinct causes — for example, irregular refuse collection, too few public waste bins, and open drains that residents misuse — and links each to observable effects such as cholera outbreaks or flooding. It then makes two actionable suggestions, such as a scheduled collection service and a community bin programme with by-law enforcement. Tone is respectful and persuasive throughout.', NULL, '["Complete formal-letter layout with addresses, date, salutation, heading and subscription","Three clearly distinguished causes of poor sanitation","Two actionable suggestions addressed to the Assembly"]', '["Specific local details naming the community","Courteous, formal register sustained to the close"]', '1');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO essay_questions (id, question_id, word_limit_min, word_limit_max, requires_introduction, requires_conclusion, marking_scheme, model_answer, marking_rubric, required_points, optional_points, ai_grading_enabled) VALUES ('eq_q_eng_2024_2_004', 'q_eng_2024_2_004', '250', '600', '1', '1', '{"points":[{"point":"Content: relevance, depth and development of ideas","marks":10},{"point":"Organization: paragraphing, coherence and logical flow","marks":10},{"point":"Expression: register, vocabulary and sentence control","marks":15},{"point":"Mechanical accuracy: spelling, punctuation and grammar","marks":5}]}', 'A strong debate speech opens with the vocative address (Chairperson, Panel of Judges, Ladies and Gentlemen) and a firm declaration of the side taken. It then advances three or four distinct arguments — for the motion: parental supervision, lower cost, freedom from hostel distractions; against it: structured prep time, independence, peer learning — each developed with an example or appeal to the audience''s experience. Rhetorical questions, rebuttal of the opposing side''s strongest claim, and a rousing conclusion mark the best speeches. Persuasive, spirited register is sustained without sliding into insult.', NULL, '["Debate format: vocative opening and clear declaration of stance","At least three distinct, developed arguments for the chosen side","A persuasive conclusion that restates the stance"]', '["A rebuttal anticipating the opposing side","Rhetorical devices: questions, repetition, direct address"]', '1');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO essay_questions (id, question_id, word_limit_min, word_limit_max, requires_introduction, requires_conclusion, marking_scheme, model_answer, marking_rubric, required_points, optional_points, ai_grading_enabled) VALUES ('eq_q_eng_2024_2_005', 'q_eng_2024_2_005', '250', '600', '1', '1', '{"points":[{"point":"Content: relevance, depth and development of ideas","marks":10},{"point":"Organization: paragraphing, coherence and logical flow","marks":10},{"point":"Expression: register, vocabulary and sentence control","marks":15},{"point":"Mechanical accuracy: spelling, punctuation and grammar","marks":5}]}', 'A strong descriptive essay appeals to at least three senses: the colours of cloth stalls and produce, the noise of bargaining and festival drums, the smells of kelewele and fresh bread. It organizes the scene spatially — entrance, food section, cloth section — or chronologically through the morning. The second half moves from description to interpretation: the scene shows communal solidarity, the importance of trade to livelihoods, or how tradition and commerce intertwine. The best answers use precise vocabulary, varied sentence openings and controlled imagery without purple overload.', NULL, '["Vivid sensory description of the market on a festival morning","An explicit explanation of what the scene reveals about the community","Coherent spatial or chronological organization"]', '["Figures of speech used sparingly and aptly","A closing reflection that ties description to meaning"]', '1');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO essay_questions (id, question_id, word_limit_min, word_limit_max, requires_introduction, requires_conclusion, marking_scheme, model_answer, marking_rubric, required_points, optional_points, ai_grading_enabled) VALUES ('eq_q_eng_2024_2_006', 'q_eng_2024_2_006', '250', '600', '1', '1', '{"points":[{"point":"Content: relevance, depth and development of ideas","marks":10},{"point":"Organization: paragraphing, coherence and logical flow","marks":10},{"point":"Expression: register, vocabulary and sentence control","marks":15},{"point":"Mechanical accuracy: spelling, punctuation and grammar","marks":5}]}', 'A strong informal letter carries the writer''s address, date, an affectionate salutation and a warm subscription with first name only. The body describes concrete club activities — weekly reading sessions, book reviews, debates on characters, author visits — and explains two benefits, such as improved vocabulary and comprehension for examinations, and confidence in public speaking from presenting reviews. The tone is friendly and enthusiastic, contractions are acceptable, and the letter ends by encouraging the friend to join or explaining how membership works.', NULL, '["Informal-letter format: one address, date, salutation and friendly subscription","A description of at least two club activities","Two clearly explained benefits of membership"]', '["Personal anecdote about the writer''s own improvement","Practical detail on how the friend can join"]', '1');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO structured_question_parts (id, question_id, part_label, part_text, marks, correct_answer, explanation, answer_type, display_order) VALUES ('sqp_q_eng_2024_2_007_a', 'q_eng_2024_2_007', 'a', 'What did Aba expect the assembly plant to bring?', 4, 'Aba expected the plant to bring only jobs (employment) to the town.', NULL, 'text', 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO structured_question_parts (id, question_id, part_label, part_text, marks, correct_answer, explanation, answer_type, display_order) VALUES ('sqp_q_eng_2024_2_007_b', 'q_eng_2024_2_007', 'b', 'State two inconveniences the town suffered when the plant first opened.', 6, 'Dust from the trucks, and the noise of trucks roaring through the market road from dawn.', NULL, 'text', 2);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO structured_question_parts (id, question_id, part_label, part_text, marks, correct_answer, explanation, answer_type, display_order) VALUES ('sqp_q_eng_2024_2_007_c', 'q_eng_2024_2_007', 'c', 'Mention three improvements the company made within a year.', 6, 'It tarred the road, drilled two boreholes, and started a night class for young apprentices.', NULL, 'text', 3);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO structured_question_parts (id, question_id, part_label, part_text, marks, correct_answer, explanation, answer_type, display_order) VALUES ('sqp_q_eng_2024_2_007_d', 'q_eng_2024_2_007', 'd', 'Why did the grumbling residents eventually soften their complaint?', 6, 'Because their children could now learn a trade at home instead of travelling to the city.', NULL, 'text', 4);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO structured_question_parts (id, question_id, part_label, part_text, marks, correct_answer, explanation, answer_type, display_order) VALUES ('sqp_q_eng_2024_2_007_e', 'q_eng_2024_2_007', 'e', 'What is the meaning of the word "apprentices" as used in the passage?', 4, 'Young people learning a trade or skill under training.', NULL, 'text', 5);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO structured_question_parts (id, question_id, part_label, part_text, marks, correct_answer, explanation, answer_type, display_order) VALUES ('sqp_q_eng_2024_2_007_f', 'q_eng_2024_2_007', 'f', 'What is the writer''s attitude towards the assembly plant?', 4, 'The writer is balanced but broadly positive, acknowledging early problems while emphasizing the benefits.', NULL, 'text', 6);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO structured_question_parts (id, question_id, part_label, part_text, marks, correct_answer, explanation, answer_type, display_order) VALUES ('sqp_q_eng_2024_2_008_a', 'q_eng_2024_2_008', 'a', 'In ONE sentence, state why urban gardening began in Accra, according to the passage.', 10, 'Urban gardening began in Accra as a response to rising food prices.', NULL, 'text', 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO structured_question_parts (id, question_id, part_label, part_text, marks, correct_answer, explanation, answer_type, display_order) VALUES ('sqp_q_eng_2024_2_008_b', 'q_eng_2024_2_008', 'b', 'In TWO sentences, summarize the economic and environmental rewards of urban gardening mentioned in the passage.', 10, 'Economically, households spend less on vegetables and can sell surplus harvests to neighbours; environmentally, rooftop beds cool buildings and the greenery softens the skyline.', NULL, 'text', 2);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO structured_question_parts (id, question_id, part_label, part_text, marks, correct_answer, explanation, answer_type, display_order) VALUES ('sqp_q_eng_2024_2_008_c', 'q_eng_2024_2_008', 'c', 'In ONE sentence, state the health and educational benefit the passage attributes to urban gardening.', 10, 'Families who grow vegetables eat them more often, and children who tend the plants learn where food comes from.', NULL, 'text', 3);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO question_content_releases (question_id, batch_id, quality_assurance, release_channel, content_label, source_url, official_exam_board_content, feedback_enabled) SELECT id, 'wassce-paper2-theory-001', 'automated_beta', 'beta', 'Original BrillaPrep curriculum-aligned WASSCE English Language Paper 2 theory practice; not official WAEC examination material. Use the enabled feedback channel to report corrections.', 'https://waecgh.org/', 0, 1 FROM questions WHERE id IN ('q_eng_2024_2_001', 'q_eng_2024_2_002', 'q_eng_2024_2_003', 'q_eng_2024_2_004', 'q_eng_2024_2_005', 'q_eng_2024_2_006', 'q_eng_2024_2_007', 'q_eng_2024_2_008');
DELETE FROM _migration_363_guard;
